  "content": {
    "id": "p20-q10-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Let $f(x)=\\dfrac{x^2}{x-1}$. Show that $f\\left(\\dfrac{x}{x-1}\\right)=f(x)$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$f\\left(\\dfrac{x}{x-1}\\right)=\\dfrac{x^2}{x-1}=f(x)$, for $x\\ne1$.",
      "worked": "For $x\\ne1$, $\\dfrac{x}{x-1}-1=\\dfrac1{x-1}\\ne0$, so the substituted argument is in the domain of $f$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}f\\left(\\frac{x}{x-1}\\right)&=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{x}{x-1}\\right)^2}{\\frac{x}{x-1}-1}\\\\f\\left(\\frac{x}{x-1}\\right)&=\\frac{x^2}{(x-1)^2}\\div\\frac1{x-1}\\\\f\\left(\\frac{x}{x-1}\\right)&=\\frac{x^2}{x-1}\\\\f\\left(\\frac{x}{x-1}\\right)&=f(x).\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 33
  },
